3
00:00:08,660 --> 00:00:09,020
Wow.

4
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:10,360
What do you mean?

5
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:11,040
What is that?

6
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:12,940
That's the move's name.

7
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:15,920
And it's running in at a diagonal.

8
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:20,760
These days, soccer teams use what's referred to as zone defense,

9
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:23,740
meaning each player is assigned to a specific area,

10
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:26,640
and so if any opponent happens to invade that space,

11
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:27,820
they're instantly tackled.

12
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:32,260
But if someone decides to run through those areas at a diagonal angle,

13
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:33,880
it confuses the defenders.

14
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:36,820
They don't know who's supposed to make the tackle

15
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:38,960
and how long they should stay on them,

16
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:40,960
or if it's worth breaking the formation.

17
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:45,180
It becomes a whole bunch of problems to solve all at once.

18
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:49,100
Still, because the diagonal run tends to be a surprise attack,

19
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,560
it doesn't seem to work well most of the time.

20
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:54,360
But somehow he did it.

21
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:57,200
Aoi's timing on this bold move was rare perfection.

22
00:00:57,820 --> 00:01:00,000
The moment he chose to run across the defenders

23
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,080
was the worst moment for them.

24
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:04,440
But how could he know that?

25
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:07,820
I bet it's because...

26
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:11,120
Now that he's been playing defense,

27
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:13,260
he understands how defenders think.

28
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:19,100
The diagonal run, it works.

29
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:20,480
It really works.

30
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:23,180
I've got so many ideas about different ways to score,

31
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:24,560
